# modules.cpp Pico platform specification

# Status and authority

This document specifies every Raspberry Pi Pico platform in this repository: the
two project-linked RAM platforms, the two Pico SDK-backed SDKs and their six
boards, the vendored Pico SDK checkout and how it is provisioned, the CMake
bridge that links against it, the C adapter and C++ wrapper that expose it, and
the MCU and USB-console providers layered over that adapter, and the smoke
applications that prove them. It describes implemented behaviour. Its manifests
require mm: 1.2.

status: official gives this document the same authority as the other implemented
specifications in docs.

docs/modules-platforms.mdy remains authoritative for what an SDK and a board are,
how a platform is selected, the processor registry's shape, responsibility
ownership, and the configuration record. docs/modules-libraries.mdy remains
authoritative for kind: library, external-build, the bridge-location convention,
bridge requirement files, checkout validation, and the external-wrapper
availability rule. docs/modules-cmake.mdy remains authoritative for the generic
external CMake bridge protocol. docs/modules-c++20.mdy remains authoritative for what a
wrapper, a linkage shim, and an adapter may contain. This document owns the Pico
instance of all of them.

Examples use Opening fence and Closing fence instead of literal front matter
delimiters, following docs/mdy.mdy.

# Two families under one directory

platforms/pico holds two kinds of platform that share a vendor and nothing else.

- rp2040-ram and rp2350-ram are minimal, project-linked, core platforms. They
  declare their own linker script, vector table, and three responsibilities, the
  project's own linker produces their executables, and they depend on no
  third-party code. They exist to prove processor arguments, responsibility
  ownership, linker scripts, and target testing without Pico SDK.
- pico, pico-w, pico2-arm, pico2-riscv, pico2-w-arm, and pico2-w-riscv are Pico
  SDK-backed. They declare no linker script, no source, and no responsibility,
  because Pico SDK's own CMake build owns startup, the runtime, the executable
  link, and the post-link images.

The second family is non-core and locked; the first is core and mutable. Keeping
them in separate subtrees is what lets one directory hold both.

# Layout

Opening fence
platforms/pico/mm.mdy                        kind: dir, name: pico
platforms/pico/apps/                         SDK-backed smoke applications
platforms/pico/rp2040-ram/                   board, linker script, vector table
platforms/pico/rp2350-ram/                   board, linker script, vector table
platforms/pico/apps/target-smoke-rp2040/     requires-board: rp2040-ram
platforms/pico/apps/target-smoke-rp2350/     requires-board: rp2350-ram

platforms/pico/sdk/mm.mdy                    kind: dir, option: core no, read-only: core
platforms/pico/sdk/pico-arm/mm.mdy           kind: sdk, arm-none-eabi
platforms/pico/sdk/pico-riscv/mm.mdy         kind: sdk, riscv32-pico-elf
platforms/pico/sdk/pico/mm.mdy               kind: board, RP2040
platforms/pico/sdk/pico-w/mm.mdy             kind: board, RP2040 with CYW43
platforms/pico/sdk/pico2-arm/mm.mdy          kind: board, RP2350 Arm Secure
platforms/pico/sdk/pico2-riscv/mm.mdy        kind: board, RP2350 RISC-V
platforms/pico/sdk/pico2-w-arm/mm.mdy        kind: board, RP2350 with CYW43, Arm Secure
platforms/pico/sdk/pico2-w-riscv/mm.mdy      kind: board, RP2350 with CYW43, RISC-V
platforms/pico/sdk/pico-sdk/mm.mdy           kind: library, name: pico-sdk
platforms/pico/sdk/pico-sdk/LICENSE.TXT      repository-owned licence copy
platforms/pico/sdk/pico-sdk/vendor.sh        provisions and pins the checkout
platforms/pico/sdk/pico-sdk/upstream/        the checkout, cloned and ignored
platforms/pico/sdk/pico-sdk/cmake/           bridge, mm-requires.txt, UF2 validation
platforms/pico/sdk/pico-sdk/ext/             adapter, shims, wrapper
platforms/pico/sdk/pico-sdk/stdio/           mm.stdio USB CDC provider

platforms/pico/apps/pico-smoke/              requires-board: pico
platforms/pico/apps/pico2-arm-smoke/         requires-board: pico2-arm
platforms/pico/apps/pico2-riscv-smoke/       requires-board: pico2-riscv
Closing fence

Everything the project authors for Pico SDK, and the vendored tree itself, lives
under platforms/pico/sdk/pico-sdk. A library that implements one platform lives
with that platform; libraries/ keeps the libraries any lane may consume.

# The non-core lock

platforms/pico/sdk/mm.mdy declares option: core no with read-only: core, and the
whole subtree inherits both: two SDKs, six board declarations, the library, and
the wrapper.

The lock is load-bearing, not decorative. The wrapper declares library: pico-sdk,
and the shared structural-property validation rejects a core node that does. With
the declaration removed the tree does not load:

Opening fence
configure: platforms/pico/sdk/pico-sdk/ext/mm.mdy: pico-ext is core, but declares library pico-sdk
Closing fence

It cannot live anywhere else. option and read-only are rejected on library, sdk,
and board manifests, so the library manifest cannot carry it, and
platforms/pico/mm.mdy would declare rp2040-ram and rp2350-ram non-core and then
lock them out of saying otherwise. The resolved records show the boundary holding:

Opening fence
platforms/pico/rp2350-ram        core: yes; default for debug; mutable
platforms/pico/sdk/pico2-arm     core: no; assigned by platforms/pico/sdk/mm.mdy; read-only
platforms/pico/sdk/pico-sdk/ext  core: no; assigned by platforms/pico/sdk/mm.mdy; read-only
Closing fence

# The SDKs

pico-arm targets gcc arm-none-eabi with newlib. pico-riscv targets gcc
riscv32-pico-elf with newlib. Both name the pico-sdk library, and both own all
five bare-metal responsibilities: reset-vector, initial-stack, memory-layout,
runtime-init, and syscalls. Neither declares a specs profile or a specs file,
which are rejected on an SDK whose library declares external-build, because the
external build owns the link and two runtimes cannot both own it.

riscv32-pico-elf is registered in the closed target-system registry as a
bare-metal target. It is also the first triple Pico SDK itself prefers, ahead of
riscv32-unknown-elf, riscv32-corev-elf, and riscv-none-elf, so the vendor field
names the Pico-targeted toolchain rather than a generic riscv32 build.

Neither SDK is named pico-sdk. A board would then declare sdk: pico-sdk while the
SDK declares library: pico-sdk, one string naming two nodes of two kinds.

# The boards

Opening fence
pico          cortex-m0plus  thumb  soft    rp2040   sdk: pico-arm
pico-w        cortex-m0plus  thumb  soft    rp2040   sdk: pico-arm
pico2-arm     cortex-m33     thumb  softfp  rp2350   sdk: pico-arm    security-domain: secure
pico2-riscv   hazard3        rv32…  soft    rp2350   sdk: pico-riscv
pico2-w-arm   cortex-m33     thumb  softfp  rp2350   sdk: pico-arm    security-domain: secure
pico2-w-riscv hazard3        rv32…  soft    rp2350   sdk: pico-riscv
rp2040-ram    cortex-m0plus  thumb  soft    rp2040   sdk: arm-none-eabi-newlib
rp2350-ram    cortex-m33     thumb  softfp  rp2350   sdk: arm-none-eabi-newlib
Closing fence

pico2-arm and pico2-riscv name the same Pico 2 hardware; pico2-w-arm and
pico2-w-riscv do the same for Pico 2 W. A board reads sdk as a required scalar
resolving to exactly one SDK, so one board cannot serve two architectures, and
each RP2350 pair is symmetric rather than one board with a variant. The RP2040
Pico and Pico W are not split because RP2040 has no RISC-V cores. Pico H and Pico
WH are header-fitted forms of Pico and Pico W and use those same SDK board names.

The six SDK-backed board declarations omit linker-script, file, and provides.
Those keys become optional only after reference resolution establishes that the
board's SDK names a library declaring external-build, which is why the
completeness check runs after every SDK and library reference resolves rather
than while a board is parsed.

boards/pico_epaper and boards/pico_epaper_b are project-level composite profiles
derived from pico. They do not add SDK boards or change the Pico bridge's vendor
vocabulary: each persisted board chain contains its composite name followed by
pico, and the bridge selects PICO_BOARD=pico from that chain. The derived boards
add only their matching mm.display providers for the monochrome and
black/white/red Waveshare Pico-ePaper-2.66 variants.

pico2-arm and pico2-w-arm declare security-domain: secure and select the Arm
Secure processor row. rp2350-ram omits the key, keeps the non-secure default,
and therefore keeps its three-argument commands unchanged.

The RISC-V boards need no new schema. cpu names the processor, hazard3.
instruction-set names the instruction set, which ARM spells thumb and RISC-V
spells as an ISA string. float-abi: soft is the soft-float integer ABI, emitted as
-mabi=ilp32. security-domain has no RISC-V counterpart and keeps its default.

## GPIO, SPI, UART, and timer surface

All six SDK-backed board declarations select platform.pico.mcu through their
SDK. The provider exposes the four portable facilities implemented for this
family:

- GPIO configure, read, and write use Pico SDK's GPIO API and validate against
  the selected chip's Bank 0 GPIO count.
- SPI instances zero and one use Pico SDK hardware_spi with caller-selected
  clock, transmit, and optional receive GPIOs. Configuration validates the
  board inventory, distinct signals, and each RP chip's SPI signal-to-GPIO mux;
  chip select remains device-driver GPIO policy.
- portable UART instance zero is the board header's PICO_DEFAULT_UART, using its
  PICO_DEFAULT_UART_TX_PIN, PICO_DEFAULT_UART_RX_PIN, and
  PICO_DEFAULT_UART_BAUD_RATE. It is initialized on first use and writes through
  hardware_uart rather than through semihosted stdio. Other portable UART
  instances answer Unsupported until the Board description can name their pin
  assignments.
- delay_ms and ticks_ms use Pico SDK's monotonic timer. The timer's epoch remains
  platform-defined, as required by mm.mcu.

The Board value reports the SDK board name and its RP GPIO inventory. Pico and
Pico 2 report their GPIO-backed LED and polarity. Pico W and Pico 2 W report no
LED: their onboard LED is attached to the CYW43 wireless device and pretending
it were an RP GPIO would violate the board-description contract. CYW43 GPIO,
Wi-Fi, and Bluetooth are not enabled merely by recognizing the W board.

## USB CDC console

Both SDKs bind mm.stdio to platform.pico.stdio at the SDK tier, so every Pico
SDK-backed board receives the USB CDC console by default and a board may replace
it through the ordinary, more-specific board-provider tier. A program that does
not use mm.stdio receives no console-provider object.

The bridge enables pico_stdio_usb and keeps semihosting enabled. The provider
initializes the Pico stdio subsystem once, then addresses the USB driver directly
for reads, writes, flushing, and connection state; it does not redirect mm.stdio
through the aggregate stdio multiplexer. The compile definition
PICO_STDIO_USB_CONNECTION_WITHOUT_DTR=1 makes connected mean that USB CDC is
enumerated and ready, rather than requiring a terminal to assert DTR. Pico SDK's
default background IRQ task services TinyUSB, so applications do not call
tud_task or another transport pump.

Pico SDK's public USB output callback does not return a byte count. Consequently
the provider reports zero bytes while disconnected and the complete size after a
connected callback returns, but cannot observe a timeout partway through that
callback. docs/modules-stdio.mdy owns the portable console contract and records
this provider-specific limitation.

# Processor arguments, and where they come from

Every argument below is read from the pinned SDK rather than inferred from a board
name.

Opening fence
cortex-m0plus thumb soft    non-secure  -mcpu=cortex-m0plus -mthumb -mfloat-abi=soft
cortex-m33    thumb softfp  non-secure  -mcpu=cortex-m33 -mthumb -mfloat-abi=softfp
cortex-m33    thumb softfp  secure      -mcpu=cortex-m33 -mthumb -mfloat-abi=softfp -mcmse
hazard3       rv32… soft    non-secure  -mcpu=hazard3-rp2350 -mstrict-align
Closing fence

For pico2-arm and pico2-w-arm, a generated compile-database entry for a Pico SDK
source contains:

Opening fence
-mcpu=cortex-m33 -mthumb -march=armv8-m.main+fp+dsp -mcmse -mfloat-abi=softfp
Closing fence

The project does not repeat the explicit -march: querying GCC shows -mcpu=cortex-m33
resolves to the same effective armv8-m.main+dsp+fp architecture. It must repeat
-mcmse, whose query state changes from disabled to enabled.

For pico2-riscv and pico2-w-riscv the SDK's own
cmake/preload/toolchains/pico_riscv_gcc.cmake offers
three flag sets in preference order and takes the first its toolchain accepts,
appending -mstrict-align to whichever wins:

Opening fence
-mcpu=hazard3-rp2350
-march=rv32ima_zicsr_zifencei_zba_zbb_zbs_zbkb_zca_zcb_zcmp -mabi=ilp32
-march=rv32imac_zicsr_zifencei_zba_zbb_zbs_zbkb -mabi=ilp32
Closing fence

The supplied Pico SDK GCC 16.1.0 toolchain accepts the first candidate, so the
registry emits that CPU profile and -mstrict-align too. Its normalised effective
architecture is
`rv32imacb_zicsr_zifencei_zmmul_zaamo_zalrsc_zca_zcb_zcmp_zba_zbb_zbkb_zbs_xh3bextm`;
that measured value is the board's instruction-set registry spelling. A toolchain
that accepts a different candidate is rejected by the ABI projection rather than
silently mixing project and SDK objects compiled for different extension sets.

# ABI projections

The project compiles its objects with the registry's arguments; Pico SDK compiles
its own with whatever its platform selects. Nothing makes those agree, so build
compares them, after CMake configure and before CMake build, over a normalised
projection obtained by asking the driver rather than by comparing spellings.

Opening fence
arm-none-eabi     -march=  -mthumb  -mfloat-abi=  -mfpu=  -mcmse
riscv32-pico-elf  -march=  -mabi=
Closing fence

-mcpu= is excluded from both. Measured on arm-none-eabi: naming a CPU reports both
-march and -mcpu, while naming the equivalent architecture reports the same -march
and leaves -mcpu empty, so comparing it would fail the equivalence the projection
exists to establish. The same reasoning excludes it for RISC-V, where the SDK may
name a CPU and the project an architecture. -mstrict-align and -mcmodel= are code
generation rather than calling convention.

The RISC-V schema was confirmed with the supplied riscv32-pico-elf GCC 16.1.0:

Opening fence
LC_ALL=C riscv32-pico-elf-gcc -mcpu=hazard3-rp2350 -mstrict-align -Q --help=target

-march= rv32imacb_zicsr_zifencei_zmmul_zaamo_zalrsc_zca_zcb_zcmp_zba_zbb_zbkb_zbs_xh3bextm
-mabi= ilp32
-mcpu= hazard3-rp2350
-mcmodel= medlow
-mstrict-align [enabled]
Closing fence

The schema retains -march and -mabi only. The driver reports both exactly once;
-mcpu is a spelling choice, while -mcmodel and strict alignment are code-generation
policy rather than calling convention. Strict parsing still makes a named field
that disappears in a future driver an error rather than allowing two empty
projections to compare equal.

# The checkout

The Pico SDK checkout is provisioned by a script, not tracked as a submodule.
platforms/pico/sdk/pico-sdk/vendor.sh is found by convention beside the library
manifest, the way the bridge is found at cmake/. It pins release 2.3.1 and commit
079c6f39023649b154152db30f1d781e884879bc, clones into upstream with the SDK's own
submodules initialised, and verifies the result.

Verification checks the repository root before the commit, requires an empty
porcelain status including submodule dirt, and requires every recursive submodule
status line to carry the leading space that means it is initialized at its recorded
gitlink. git rev-parse inside an empty or non-repository directory answers for
whichever repository encloses it, so without the root check an unprovisioned
upstream reports this project's own HEAD and compares unequal for the wrong reason.

- With a clean checkout and all recursive submodules at the pinned revisions the
  script prints so and exits 0.
- With it at another commit the script exits 65 naming both commits and does not
  modify anything.
- With the top-level commit pinned but the checkout dirty or a recursive submodule
  absent or drifted, it exits 65 and names that state.
- With it absent the script clones, then verifies, and fails the same way if the
  tag no longer resolves to the pinned commit.

The recursive checkout is about 800 MB and carries six nested gitlinks: btstack,
cyw43-driver, lwip, mbedtls with its own framework, and tinyusb. None is needed by
this integration, which links pico_stdlib with semihosting stdio; they are
initialised because the SDK pins them and a partial checkout is a second thing to
reason about.

upstream is ignored, and clean.sh keeps its out/, out-*/, gcm.cache/ scope, so
cleaning never discards a checkout that costs a clone to restore. The licence is
copied to LICENSE.TXT outside the checkout, so an unprovisioned tree stays
inspectable and valid.

An absent checkout is reported by naming that script, derived from the library
manifest's own directory:

Opening fence
configure: library pico-sdk checkout is absent: platforms/pico/sdk/pico-sdk/upstream; run 'platforms/pico/sdk/pico-sdk/vendor.sh'
Closing fence

A script pin is weaker than a gitlink: the parent repository neither verifies it
nor shows drift. Re-running the script checks the ignored checkout itself, and the
tag and commit recorded here are the second record of the pin.

# The bridge

platforms/pico/sdk/pico-sdk/cmake/CMakeLists.txt is project-authored and owns
everything Pico-specific. Build hands it generic values only: the ordered project
objects, the requested output name, the library's source path, the selected
board's name, and the compiler drivers. Nothing in mm.build names Pico.

The order matters and is deliberate: inputs are read, the board is mapped, and only
then does Pico SDK see either.

Opening fence
include(${CMAKE_CURRENT_BINARY_DIR}/mm-inputs.cmake)
walk MM_BOARD_CHAIN -> MM_VENDOR_BOARD -> PICO_BOARD, PICO_PLATFORM
include(${MM_LIBRARY_SOURCE}/pico_sdk_init.cmake)
project(... C CXX ASM)
pico_sdk_init()
Closing fence

The board mapping walks MM_BOARD_CHAIN, takes the first recognised vendor board,
and lives only here because only CMake needs it:

Opening fence
pico          PICO_BOARD=pico    PICO_PLATFORM=rp2040
pico-w        PICO_BOARD=pico_w  PICO_PLATFORM=rp2040
pico2-arm     PICO_BOARD=pico2   PICO_PLATFORM=rp2350-arm-s
pico2-riscv   PICO_BOARD=pico2   PICO_PLATFORM=rp2350-riscv
pico2-w-arm   PICO_BOARD=pico2_w PICO_PLATFORM=rp2350-arm-s
pico2-w-riscv PICO_BOARD=pico2_w PICO_PLATFORM=rp2350-riscv
Closing fence

PICO_BOARD is the same within each RP2350 hardware pair because it names the
board; PICO_PLATFORM is what selects the architecture. A chain containing no
recognised board is a fatal error naming the entire chain: this bridge requires
one, even though generic external linking does not. The bridge passes
`MM_SELECTED_BOARD="${MM_BOARD}"` via compile definitions to the C adapter so
runtime board identification (`mm::mcu::board().name`) reports the selected
board rather than `PICO_BOARD`.

The target is the fixed internal name mm_external, so no manifest-derived name
reaches a CMake identifier; the requested name arrives only as OUTPUT_NAME. Project
objects carry EXTERNAL_OBJECT and GENERATED so CMake does not recompile them, and
they are listed in order because self-registering static initialisers depend on it.
LINKER_LANGUAGE CXX is set explicitly: the only ordinary source in the target is
one C file, and without it CMake may drive the link with the C compiler.

The bridge reports two things back through generated files: mm-abi-probe.txt names
the source whose compile-database entry build projects, and mm-result.txt names the
primary artifact and its supplements.

# picotool

Pico SDK 2.3.1 may otherwise fetch and build picotool while configuring extra
outputs. The bridge therefore carries a checked-in cmake/mm-requires.txt whose one
line is picotool_DIR. This is the generic bridge-requirements contract, not a check
on the library name.

Before invoking CMake, build requires that variable to name an existing directory
containing an existing regular file named either picotoolConfig.cmake or
picotool-config.cmake, the two spellings find_package accepts. It canonicalises the
directory, passes it as picotool_DIR:PATH, and fingerprints its path and contents
into the external cache identity. It does not execute the tool or parse the package
file to reach that decision.

Opening fence
build: external bridge requires CMake package variable picotool_DIR; it is unset (expected picotoolConfig.cmake or picotool-config.cmake)
Closing fence

CMake configuration also sets FETCHCONTENT_FULLY_DISCONNECTED=ON as a second guard,
because CMake's own documentation says that flag assumes dependencies are already
populated and is not a first-run network guard. The guard is build refusing to
configure. The installed package remains responsible for satisfying the SDK's
required picotool version; an incompatible one fails in the SDK's config-mode
find_package rather than falling back to a download.

After picotool runs, the bridge validates every 512-byte block of the generated UF2
against both leading magic words and the trailing magic word. A missing, empty,
mis-sized, or incorrectly framed output fails the external build before publication
and is removed from the external cache. Supplying a package stub is therefore not
evidence that UF2 production works.

All six board declarations were built with picotool 2.3.1. The published images
pass the bridge validator and `picotool info` identifies their family IDs as
rp2040, rp2350-arm-s, and rp2350-riscv respectively; the RP2350 reports further
identify the images as Arm Secure and RISC-V.

The separate flash operation consumes one of those published UF2 supplements.
tools/flash validates the retained Pico target and runs `picotool load -v -x`;
flash.sh supplies the repository-local picotool package location when the
environment does not. docs/modules-flash.mdy owns the programming contract.

# The wrapper boundary

No project module includes pico/stdlib.h. That header aggregates generated
configuration headers, board headers, hardware headers, compile definitions, and
transitive CMake targets, none of which a library include interface can carry.

ext/adapter.c is the only file that includes Pico SDK headers. It is compiled by
the bridge inside Pico SDK's own target graph, with whatever flags that graph
selects, so one C source serves all six board declarations and both
architectures. It implements the wrapper ABI, platform.pico.mcu's private GPIO,
hardware-SPI, hardware-UART, timer, and board-description ABI, and
platform.pico.stdio's private USB-console ABI. The bridge requests GNU C11
rather than strict C11 because Pico SDK's public Hazard3 headers use GNU `asm`;
the adapter itself uses no language extension, but it must be parsed together
with the foreign headers it includes.

ext/pico-c.h declares that ABI in plain C, with no language linkage specifier of
its own, because adapter.c compiles as C and the wrapper as C++. ext/pico-cxx.h is
the colocated C++ linkage shim that wraps it in extern "C", which is how the
wrapper obtains the linkage without an __cplusplus test: docs/modules-c++20.mdy
permits no preprocessor directive other than #include.

ext/pico.cppm exports module lib.pico with typed C++ functions over that ABI. Its
manifest name is pico-ext, so that pico names two nodes rather than three.

The wrapper's library: pico-sdk declaration contributes nothing to any compile
command. It requires the checkout when a closure reaches the wrapper, it drives the
availability rule, and it records the dependency in the manifest tree and the
model, but its include interface is empty. The external build is what makes Pico
SDK work, not include propagation.

lib.pico is available only in a target lane whose selected SDK names pico-sdk, so
it compiles in the pico-arm and pico-riscv lanes and is reported as a skipped
unavailable target everywhere else, including an arm-none-eabi lane with rp2040-ram
selected.

adapter.c is project-authored production code that no manifest declares, so it is
invisible to the source inventory, cppcheck, model, and document. The bridge
compiles it with an explicit strict C dialect and warnings as errors, every smoke
application references every ABI entry point so a removed function fails the link,
and its review is a named acceptance condition.

# The smoke applications

Opening fence
mm: 1.2
kind: app
name: pico2-arm-smoke
option: core no
option: buildable-host no
requires-board: pico2-arm
use: lib.pico
file: main.cpp
Closing fence

All three declarations are load-bearing. core no because the application uses
lib.pico, which is non-core, and that is a declared dependency edge the core rule
governs. buildable-host no because requires-board is not consulted in the host
lane. requires-board because it is what makes the application unavailable in every
other target lane, which the wrapper's availability rule depends on: an application
whose closure reaches an external wrapper must be pinned to a lane where that
wrapper is available.

# Build and publication

1. Resolve the lane, SDK, board, responsibilities, and checkout, and compare the
   record's link ownership with the manifest's.
2. Compile project modules and the application with the lane compiler and the
   registry's processor arguments.
3. Create or validate the external directory for this lane and executable.
4. Generate mm-inputs.cmake and mm-toolchain.cmake, and configure the bridge with
   the pinned generator, the recorded C, C++, and ASM drivers, and picotool_DIR.
5. Read the bridge's ABI probe, project both sides, and fail on any difference.
6. Build the CMake target and validate the UF2.
7. Read mm-result.txt and publish.

The primary artifact is published at the ordinary artifact path, extensionless, so
run and debug reach it exactly as they reach a project-linked executable.
Supplements are published beside it as the same name plus their own suffix:

Opening fence
out-target-arm-none-eabi/platforms/pico/apps/pico-smoke/pico-smoke        the ELF
out-target-arm-none-eabi/platforms/pico/apps/pico-smoke/pico-smoke.bin
out-target-arm-none-eabi/platforms/pico/apps/pico-smoke/pico-smoke.hex
out-target-arm-none-eabi/platforms/pico/apps/pico-smoke/pico-smoke.uf2
out-target-arm-none-eabi/platforms/pico/apps/pico-smoke/pico-smoke.elf.map
Closing fence

External linking is lane-wide. Every available application in an SDK-backed lane is
handed to the bridge, whether or not its closure reaches the wrapper, so a portable
core application builds there too and is linked by the platform it was going to be
linked against anyway.

The external directory is out-target-<target>/external/pico-sdk/<board>/<executable>.
Its cache identity covers the bridge directory's contents, both generated files,
the generator, CMake, the drivers and their versions, picotool, the sysroot, the
system name, the processor arguments, the ABI projection, the board, the output
name, and the build type. A mismatch clears and reconfigures; nothing stale is
reused.

# Operational notes

Three behaviours surprise people and are recorded here rather than rediscovered.

- Renaming a selected SDK or board invalidates a configuration record, which stores
  both names and both manifest paths. The stale record blocks configure as well as
  build, because configure loads the existing record before writing a new one, so
  the recovery is to remove out/config.mdy and configure again.
- Adding a target triple is a two-step build, because the tool that validates the
  tree is built from the tree. The registry change must be compiled into out/bin
  before a manifest declaring the new triple is registered; a single-step attempt
  fails with unsupported target from the previously installed binary.
- option and read-only are rejected on kind: board, so a board can never declare
  its own core value. A board's core value is inherited, and the resolved records
  under the lane build directory are where to read it.

# Conformance

- A clean bootstrap and an ordinary host build succeed with the checkout absent,
  and neither reaches lib.pico.
- rp2040-ram and rp2350-ram build with the checkout absent, and their compile and
  link commands are unchanged by any of the Pico SDK work.
- lib.pico is reported as a skipped unavailable target in every lane except one
  whose selected SDK names pico-sdk.
- Removing the lock from platforms/pico/sdk/mm.mdy fails the load, naming the
  wrapper and the library. rp2350-ram remains core and mutable.
- vendor.sh clones, verifies the top-level pin and recursive checkout state, is a
  no-op when correct, and exits 65 when HEAD, tracked content, or a nested checkout
  has moved. A moved HEAD diagnostic names both commits. clean.sh does not remove
  the checkout.
- An absent checkout names vendor.sh and no git command.
- With picotool_DIR unset, every SDK-backed Pico board fails before CMake is
  invoked, naming the variable and both accepted file names.
- No source beneath the checkout reaches the project compiler or cppcheck, on a
  whole-tree check and on a check rooted at platforms.
- pico-arm, pico-riscv, pico, pico-w, pico2-arm, pico2-riscv, pico2-w-arm, and
  pico2-w-riscv all resolve by name; the superseded pico-sdk-arm and pico2
  resolve nowhere.
- A record written after the rename names pico-arm and pico2-arm.
- The ABI projections of both sides are compared between CMake configure and CMake
  build; an equivalent ABI spelled differently passes.
- Project objects reach the link in declaration order, are not recompiled by CMake,
  and the final link is performed by the recorded C++ driver.
- All six SDK-backed board declarations link with a real picotool 2.3.1 package.
  Their published artifacts appear at the ordinary paths with the documented
  suffixes, their UF2 family IDs match their platforms, and a declared artifact
  missing after the build is an external-build failure.
- The adapter for each board compiles GPIO, both hardware SPI instances, the
  default hardware UART, and monotonic timer support. W-board builds report no
  GPIO-backed LED.
- A program using mm.stdio receives exactly one platform.pico.stdio provider in
  either Pico SDK lane. A program that does not use mm.stdio receives none.
- The Pico console initializes the SDK stdio subsystem without an application
  task loop, uses USB CDC connection state without requiring DTR, and leaves
  semihosting enabled beside USB.

# Current boundaries

The GPIO edge latch uses the SDK's generic GPIO callback on core zero. Code
using another GPIO interrupt while watches exist must not call
`gpio_set_irq_enabled_with_callback`, which would replace that callback.
It may use `gpio_add_raw_irq_handler_masked`, or the 64-bit masked variant
for RP2350 GPIOs above 31, on disjoint pins only. Raw handlers remove their
pins from generic callback dispatch; a raw handler and a watch cannot own
the same pin. The adapter cannot detect that conflict.
Watches run on core zero only; all four edge calls on core one answer
Unsupported. The generic callback and bank IRQ remain installed after the
last unwatch so a later watch can use them again.

- Nothing automated asserts that a Pico image ran. The OpenOCD profile programs,
  verifies, and resets, but defines no completion protocol, which is the same limit
  docs/modules-platforms.mdy records for target test execution. Hardware execution
  is an opt-in script whose result a person reads.
- Which OpenOCD machine value the RP2350 RISC-V boards should carry is unsettled.
  Their RISC-V debug path is not the ARM one, and no runner profile is claimed
  for it.
- RP2350 RISC-V hardware has not been exercised at all.
- CYW43 GPIO and firmware, Wi-Fi, lwIP, Bluetooth, TinyUSB classes beyond the
  USB CDC console, flash boot policy, and signed images are outside this
  integration, though their sources are present in the recursive checkout.
  Pico W and Pico 2 W are supported for the same RP GPIO, hardware SPI,
  hardware UART, and timer surface as the non-wireless boards.
- picotool is required, not vendored. A hermetic image pipeline would vendor it.
- Direct project-module use of Pico SDK headers waits for a library interface that
  can carry generated include directories and compile definitions as typed data.
